# Human Capital Materiality Readiness Before the 10-K

Human capital materiality does not begin when a company drafts its 10-K.

It begins earlier, when workforce systems start producing incomplete evidence: AI productivity metrics that do not show verification labor, retention data that records exits after capacity has already eroded, and performance data that cannot distinguish human capability from AI-assisted output.

By the time those risks appear in annual reporting, the governance opportunity may already have passed.

---

## What the SEC Requires — and What AI Is Breaking

Since 2020, the SEC has required public companies to disclose human capital resources material to understanding the business. The rule does not prescribe specific metrics. It requires disclosure of whatever a reasonable investor would consider important to understanding how the company manages its workforce.

That was a manageable disclosure problem when human capital data was relatively stable — headcount, turnover, training investment, compensation structure. The underlying systems produced readable data. The board reviewed it. Counsel determined what was material. The 10-K reflected it.

AI adoption has broken that chain at the source.

The underlying systems are no longer producing readable data. Productivity metrics that were built to measure human output now measure a combination of human judgment and AI generation that the system cannot separate. Retention data records when people leave but not whether the institutional knowledge required to verify AI output left with them. Performance data reflects completed work but cannot show whether the human who signed off had the time, tools, and authority to evaluate what the AI produced.

The board is reviewing data that was accurate when the systems were built and is no longer a reliable picture of the workforce's actual capacity, risk exposure, or readiness.

---

## Three Specific Failures

**AI productivity metrics that exclude verification labor.**

When a company reports that AI has increased output by a measurable percentage, that figure typically counts what was produced. It does not count the human time required to verify it. In regulated industries, in legal and compliance functions, in financial reporting, verification is not optional — it is the governance control. A productivity metric that excludes verification labor is not reporting efficiency. It is reporting volume while concealing the cost and capacity required to make that volume reliable.

The Mayo Clinic lawsuit filed July 6, 2026 makes this failure concrete in a healthcare context. Traci Tamiko Eto, the clinic's former Director of Research Operations, alleged that her supervisor dismissed concerns about bypassed institutional review board (IRB) processes because additional review "would jeopardize the pace of ongoing research projects" and "compromise Mayo's competitive advantage." The IRB review is verification labor. When that labor was excluded from the institution's operational calculation, the output continued — but the governance control that made the output trustworthy had been removed. The productivity metric would not have shown that.

The same failure appeared in the SEC's Presto Automation enforcement action. Presto reported non-intervention rates of 95 to 99 percent — but those figures excluded offshore human agents who were entering orders in the Philippines and India. The metric counted output without counting the labor required to produce it. The result was a disclosure that looked like efficiency data and was actually concealment.

This is the data problem Article 02 in this series identified at the capital level: AI investment is scaling faster than human capacity. The human capital data failure is what makes that gap invisible until it becomes a disclosure problem.

**Retention data that records exits after capacity has already eroded.**

Standard retention reporting captures when an employee leaves. It does not capture what leaves with them. In an AI-assisted workflow, the humans who understand the limits of the system — who know when the model is wrong, when the output needs escalation, when the verification step cannot be skipped — are not interchangeable with the humans who replaced them.

When those people leave, the organization's actual capacity to govern its AI output declines before any system records it. The retention data shows a number. The number does not show that the verification layer just got thinner.

In the Mayo Clinic case, Eto oversaw a team of 36 employees responsible for the institution's human research protection and IRB operations. The lawsuit alleges she was demoted, stripped of leadership responsibilities, and ultimately fired after raising AI compliance concerns. Her departure would appear in retention data as a single director-level exit. It represented the loss of the governance function responsible for ensuring AI research met federal standards — and a signal to 36 staff members about what happens to people who raise compliance concerns.

**Performance data that cannot distinguish human from AI-assisted output.**

Performance systems were designed to evaluate human work. They are increasingly evaluating the combination of human judgment and AI generation as a single output, with no record of which decisions the human made, what the AI produced, and where the human's role was substantive versus confirmatory.

That creates a board-level evidence problem. If a regulator, investor, or plaintiff asks what the responsible human actually did — what they reviewed, what they approved, what they had the capacity to evaluate — the performance record may not be able to answer. The human is named. The accountability is not documented.

The Mayo Clinic lawsuit alleges that a study of MAYA, the clinic's AI-powered digital assistant, mischaracterized patient outcomes, deleted unfavorable data, and used unauthorized software. Ten separate whistleblower complaints raised similar concerns, including allegations that researchers sought to conceal a 67 percent error rate. The study was approved and exempted from IRB inspection. The performance record would show an approved research output. It would not show that the humans who signed off had suppressed contradictory findings rather than evaluated them.

This is the disclosure-control problem Article 05 identified from the GC's perspective. At the human capital level, it means the workforce data that should substantiate accountability claims does not contain the evidence required to support them.

