## Why the Governance Window Closes Before the 10-K

Annual reporting is a rear-view mirror. By the time human capital risks appear in a 10-K — through a material weakness finding, an SEC comment letter, a restatement, or investor scrutiny of workforce disclosures — the decisions that created those risks are already in the past.

The board that learns about verification labor gaps from an annual disclosure cycle is the board that has been approving AI productivity claims without a reliable basis for months or years. The board that learns about retention risk at the point of exit has already lost the institutional knowledge required to course-correct.

Readiness means having the governance data before it becomes a disclosure problem — not after.

The three data failures above are not disclosure failures yet. They are governance failures that will become disclosure failures if the board cannot answer, with evidence, the questions a reasonable investor would ask: What is the actual human capacity behind your AI-assisted output? What does your workforce verification layer look like? How do you know the humans named as accountable had what they needed to exercise real judgment?

