## What the Name Standard℠ Adds to Human Capital Readiness

The Name Standard℠ was built for exactly the gap between human capital data and human capital accountability.

It tests whether the humans named as accountable behind AI-assisted output actually had the capacity, information, authority, documentation, and escalation path required to make that accountability real — not ceremonial.

The Mayo Clinic case illustrates what happens when the fifth pillar is missing. The Name Standard℠ requires a Formal Right of Refusal: the institutional standing and explicit authority to halt, escalate, or document non-compliant AI assets without fear of internal reprisal. Eto had the title, the mandate, and the compliance expertise. The lawsuit alleges she did not have the protected escalation path. When she reported concerns to Mayo's legal department, she was excluded from leadership meetings and targeted for removal. The governance function she represented was not empowered — it was eliminated.

The Presto Automation enforcement action produced the same finding at the corporate disclosure level. Presto executives flagged the misleading AI terminology internally in October 2022 and again in January 2023. No corrective action followed because no formal process existed to require it. The SEC's third violation in the order was the absence of disclosure controls and procedures — no one formally responsible for ensuring the company's filings were accurate.

Both cases show the same failure: the human who knew was not empowered to act, and the organization had no formal structure requiring the knowledge to reach the governance decision.

Applied to human capital readiness, the Name Standard℠ asks five questions before the 10-K drafting process begins:

Do workforce productivity metrics separate AI-generated volume from verified, human-reviewed output?

Does the organization know which roles carry verification responsibility for AI-assisted work — and whether those roles are adequately staffed?

When a human approves AI output, is there a record showing they had the time, tools, and access required to evaluate it?

When key employees in verification or oversight roles leave, does the organization track what governance capacity departed with them?

Can the board produce, on request, the evidence trail showing human accountability behind AI-assisted decisions — not a description of the process, but the actual record?

If the answer to any of those questions is unclear, the organization is carrying human capital risk that its current data cannot see — and that its next annual filing will not be able to explain.

